## Output Folders

Screenshots go in `screenshots/`, plans/PRDs/specs in `plans/`, handoff docs in `handoffs/`, QA reports in `qa-report/` (all at repo root).

## Communication Style

<!-- Optional. Delete this section to keep normal verbosity. -->
Caveman mode always active (`.claude/skills/caveman/SKILL.md`). Terse, no filler, full accuracy. Off only on "stop caveman" or "normal mode".

## Permissions

Always confirm before: `rm`, `del`, `rmdir`, `Remove-Item`, `git rm`, `git reset`, `git push`, `git checkout`, `git rebase`. Never bypass.

## Verification

Evidence before claims. No "done", "fixed", "passing", or "works" — including paraphrases or anything implying success — without having run the proving command this turn and read its output. A subagent's success report is a claim to verify against the VCS diff, not proof.

This is a rule about what you may *claim*, not an instruction to re-check your work. Correcting yourself while writing is not the same as having run the thing; nothing substitutes for the command and its output.

Red flags you're about to violate this: "should work now", "looks right", satisfaction before evidence ("Great!", "Perfect!"), or committing/pushing without a fresh run.

Where the workflow assigns proof to the user (e.g. the pure-style eyeball), say "applied, awaiting your eyes" — that is not a done claim.

## Scope, Delegation, Length

**Scope.** Deliver what was asked, at the scope intended. Make routine judgment calls yourself; check in only when different readings of the request lead to materially different work. If the request looks mistaken or a better approach exists, say so in a sentence and build what was asked — never quietly narrow, widen, or transform it. Finish the whole task, and stop short of anything clearly beyond it.

**Delegation.** Subagents are for large, genuinely independent work: a wide multi-file investigation, or a read nobody has done yet (the cold spec review). Never spawn one to double-check work you just did — a second look at the same code with the same knowledge buys nothing. One agent beats several; keep spawn counts low.

**Length.** Match written documents — PRDs, plans, issue files, handoffs, reports — to what the task needs. Cover the substance, then stop. No filler sections, restated summaries, or boilerplate. Long is not thorough.

## Lanes

Not every task is feature work; route before starting. Pick the lane by observable predicate, never by how small the work feels. When the lane is anything but Feature, state the lane and its predicate in one line before starting. If the predicate flips mid-work (schema gets touched, new behavior appears), stop and upgrade lanes — never finish in the wrong lane.

| Lane | Predicate | Route |
|---|---|---|
| Feature | Adds user-visible behavior, touches schema, or opens a new module — however small it feels | Workflow below, from step 1 |
| Quick fix | Behavior-preserving or cosmetic; no schema; confined to one area | Fix → `/verify-feature` if behavior was touched (pure style: user eyeball) → `/code-review` if non-trivial → commit on a feature branch. No issue file, no spec review — there is no spec to check |
| Advisory | No working-tree change intended: audit, ideas, roadmap | `/improve` or `/improve-codebase-architecture`; output is plans, no gates |
| Promote | User picks an advisory plan or finding to build | Enter the workflow at `/to-issues` with the plan path — the plan already is the PRD; don't re-grill it |
| Polish | Visual pass on existing UI | `/visual-spec` or `/mobile-view` loops; user-eyeball rule applies |
| Debug | Something is broken | Investigate first; the fix then re-routes by predicate (usually Quick fix) |

No lane relaxes: Verification, Permissions, the default-branch guard, or `/track`.

## Workflow (Feature lane)

1. `/grill-me` (new idea) or `/grill-with-docs` (touches existing domain concepts); a handoff doc or brief feeds the grill, answering most questions upfront
2. `/frontend-design` first for any new UI (establish the visual language before design work; record what it establishes in `/visual-spec`'s auto-resolved table so later sections inherit it); `/prototype` (optional); `/visual-spec` for UI work (lock visuals before markup/CSS)
3. `/to-prd` then `/to-issues`
4. `/implement` per issue. Chains the gates in order: build (`/tdd` for complex logic, `/new-migration` for schema) → `/verify-feature` (behavior changes; pure style skips the browser, user eyeballs) → review (fresh-context spec-compliance subagent, then a quick `/code-review`; the deep quality pass runs once per branch in `/finish-branch`) → delete issue file → commit on the feature branch
5. `/finish-branch` when the branch is done: CI trio green, docs sweep, merge in the layout's required order, cleanup, then `/sharpen` (workflow friction review; cheapest-fix-first, new skills only on repeat evidence); after any push, `/watch-ci` offers to babysit Actions until green

`/track` auto-triggers on any plan with 5+ steps: checklist in `.claude/tasks/active.md`, survives context compression, no "done" until every box checked, archive on completion. `/qa-sweep` for full-app QA runs.
